# machine/thoughts.pair.math -- WIRE 5 seed questions, the prime-pair chart.
#
# Read with:  runghc -imachine machine/MathMachine.hs --pair --thoughts machine/thoughts.pair.math --rounds N
#
# The engine can in principle GENERATE these, but not cheaply: the split
# norm identity is a size-7 term and its horizon starts at 4.  Seeding is
# the researcher-input path THOUGHT_FORMAT.md documents; a seeded candidate
# "receives no privileged proof status" -- it must still agree on every
# fingerprint, still be proved, and still pass the same Agda kernel gate.
#
# leglo(w,r) = w -. r,  leghi(w,r) = w + r,  splitnorm(w,r) = w*w -. r*r
# over N with truncated subtraction (PairVocab section 3c / section 10).
#
# THE TARGET, first line: the split norm identity, the one of
# PairCoordinates.agda three ring identities that survives truncation.
candidate	*(leglo(x,y),leghi(x,y))	splitnorm(x,y)
candidate	leglo(x,0)	x
candidate	leghi(x,0)	x
candidate	leghi(0,x)	x
candidate	leglo(x,x)	0
candidate	splitnorm(x,x)	0
candidate	splitnorm(x,0)	*(x,x)
candidate	leghi(x,y)	leghi(y,x)
candidate	leglo(leghi(x,y),y)	x
candidate	leglo(leghi(x,y),x)	y
candidate	leghi(leglo(x,y),y)	max(x,y)
candidate	leglo(x,leghi(x,y))	0
candidate	splitnorm(x,leghi(x,y))	0
candidate	leglo(leglo(x,y),z)	leglo(x,leghi(y,z))
candidate	leghi(leghi(x,y),z)	leghi(x,leghi(y,z))
candidate	leglo(x,s(x))	0
candidate	le(leglo(x,y),x)	s(0)
candidate	le(x,leghi(x,y))	s(0)
# NEGATIVE CONTROLS.  Both are TRUE over Z (PairCoordinates.agda proves
# them over any commutative ring) and FALSE over N off the cone r <= w.
# They must be dropped by the fingerprint filter BEFORE any proof attempt;
# if either ever reaches the prover this seed file is lying.
candidate	+(leglo(x,y),leghi(x,y))	*(s(s(0)),x)
candidate	-(*(leghi(x,y),leghi(x,y)),*(*(s(s(s(s(0)))),leglo(x,y)),leghi(x,y)))	*(s(s(s(s(0)))),*(y,y))
# WHY LINE 1 IS STILL OPEN, and it is not the vocabulary.
#
# leglo(x,y)*leghi(x,y) = splitnorm(x,y) -- i.e. (x -. y)*(x + y) = x*x -. y*y
# over N -- reaches the prover on every run and comes back attempted-no-proof,
# including at --nested-depth 3.  The reason is structural and is in monus, not
# in the pair chart:
#
#   `-` is the only symbol in this vocabulary whose defining equations recurse
#   on BOTH arguments at once (MathMachine.hs baseVocabulary: -(x,0) = x,
#   -(0,x) = 0, -(s x, s y) = -(x,y)).  proveByInduction (MathMachine.hs
#   ~1280) substitutes s# for exactly ONE variable in the step case, so in a
#   step case built from a two-variable monus term the other argument is still
#   a bare variable and NO monus clause can fire.  Induction on x leaves
#   -(s#, y); induction on y leaves -(x, s#); both are irreducible.  Nested
#   induction does not help, because the inner induction inherits the same
#   one-variable substitution.
#
# So the split norm needs either a prover that splits a PAIR of variables into
# (0,y) / (x,0) / (s x, s y), or a lemma chain that removes the two-variable
# monus first -- the natural one is right-distributivity (x -. y)*z =
# x*z -. y*z together with cancellation (a + c) -. (c + b) = a -. b, and the
# first of those is blocked by the identical obstruction.  That is the next
# piece of work, and it is a prover, not a vocabulary.
